---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import StitchStudy from '../../components/studio/StitchStudy.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/urls';
---
<Layout standalone title="From drawing to hand stitches" description="Turn a drawing into running stitches and explore the spaces between them.">
  <article class="stitch-tutorial">
    <div class="step-screen" id="study-screen">
      <p><a href={withBase('/studio/')}>← Studio</a></p>
      <StitchStudy />
    </div>
    <section class="page prose stitch-notes">
      <h2>What to try</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Start with the petal medallion. Notice that the centre stays open.</li>
        <li>Shorten the stitches, then make the gaps wider. How does the line change?</li>
        <li>Choose the flower. Its stem is a separate path, so it is never joined to the flower by an extra line.</li>
        <li>Try the horse and compare its curves and corners.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Use Next to move from the drawing to the stitched outline and pattern fill. Preview opens the animation. Prev keeps your settings; Reset starts again.</p>
      <h2>From the work</h2>
      <p>The geometric medallion is a new study inspired by the drawing guide and hand stitching in <a href={withBase('/artwork/instagram-CWO3doINYa7-01/')}>this piece by Emilie →</a>. The flower and horse are also study drawings, rather than tracings of original artworks.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
<style>
  .stitch-tutorial { width: 100%; min-width: 0; margin-inline: auto; }
  .step-screen { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-height: 0; height: calc(var(--studio-viewport-height, 100vh) - 2 * var(--standalone-padding-y)); }
  .step-screen > p { flex-shrink: 0; margin: 0 0 .5rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  .stitch-notes { margin: calc((4.2rem + clamp(.5rem, 2vw, 1.5rem)) / 3) 0 0; padding-top: 0; }
  .stitch-notes > h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
